Answer: Java allows any number of underscores between digits. Example:

```java
int a = 1_0;
int b = 1_____0;
```

Both variables store the value 10.

Answer: No. An underscore cannot be placed before the first digit or after the last digit. Invalid examples:

```java
int a = _10;
int b = 10_;
```

An underscore must be placed only between digits.

Answer: Underscores can be placed: Between digits before the decimal point Between digits after the decimal point Examples:

```java
float a = 3_3.147F;
float b = 33.1_47F;
```

The underscores do not affect the value.

Answer: An underscore cannot be placed: Immediately before the decimal point Immediately after the decimal point Immediately before the suffix After the suffix At the beginning or end of the literal Invalid examples:

```java
float a = 33_.147F;
float b = 33._147F;
float c = 33.147_F;
float d = 33.147F_;
```

Underscores must appear only between digits.

Answer: A real number can be represented in two ways: Normal decimal notation Scientific notation Example:

```java
double a = 3147.326;
double b = 3.147326E3;
```

Both statements represent the same value.

Answer: ```java
class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
double a = 3147.326;
double b = 3.147326E3;
double c = 314732.6E-2;
System.out.println(a);
System.out.println(b);
System.out.println(c);
}
}
```

Output: 3147.326 3147.326 3147.326 All three literals represent the same real number. The first uses normal notation, while the second and third use scientific notation.

Answer: No. The L suffix is not mandatory when the literal is within the range of int. Examples:

```java
long a = 0;
long b = 128;
long c = 32768;
long d = 2147483647;
```

The values are initially treated as int and then implicitly widened to long.
